---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO_URL, SITE } from '../lib/site';

const siteUrl = SITE.url.replace(/\/$/, '');

const orgLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Organization',
  name: 'LocalCertKit',
  url: `${siteUrl}/`,
  description:
    'Browser-local certificate and key tools: PFX to PEM conversion, certificate and CSR decoding, key matching and chain ordering. Files are never uploaded.',
  sameAs: [REPO_URL],
};
---

<Base
  title="About: why CertKit exists"
  description="CertKit is a set of browser-local certificate and key tools built on one rule: private keys never leave your device. RSA-first v1, EC support on the roadmap."
  jsonLd={[orgLd]}
>
  <div class="wrap tool-wrap">
    <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li aria-current="page">About</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <header class="tool-head">
      <h1>About CertKit</h1>
      <p class="lead">Certificate chores shouldn’t require uploading private keys to a stranger’s server.</p>
    </header>

    <section class="card" data-reveal>
      <h2>Why this exists</h2>
      <p>
        Converting a PFX, decoding a CSR or checking whether a key matches a certificate are small,
        routine jobs, the kind that should take seconds. Yet almost every free tool online asks you
        to paste or upload the exact files you are trying to protect. CertKit takes the other
        position: the tools are static pages, all processing happens in your browser, and the claims
        are verifiable with DevTools.
      </p>

      <h2>Principles</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Local by default.</strong> No upload, no server, no storage.</li>
        <li><strong>Honest limits.</strong> v1 is RSA-first; EC/Ed25519 objects are reported as unsupported rather than mishandled, and the PEM → PFX writer documents its SHA-1 MAC and unencrypted certificate bags instead of hiding them.</li>
        <li><strong>No business model aimed at your data.</strong> No ads, no accounts, no personal tracking. The only analytics is a cookie-less Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon for anonymous visit counts (see the <a href="/privacy/#visit-counts">privacy note</a>). A single affiliate slot exists in the configuration for a possible future certificate-provider partner; it is unused in v1 and labeled as such.</li>
        <li><strong>Tested, not vibes.</strong> Every claim on this site has a fixture and a test behind it, run against OpenSSL ground truth where possible.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How it is built</h2>
      <p>
        Static pages (Astro), zero backend, and one vendored crypto/ASN.1 library, node-forge 1.4.0,
        served from this domain. No CDNs, no fonts fetched from anywhere, and exactly one third-party
        script: the cookie-less Cloudflare Web Analytics beacon that counts visits. The
        test suite runs the same code in Node and in a real headless browser, with PKCS#12, PEM, DER
        and JWK fixtures generated by OpenSSL and checked back against it.
      </p>

      <h2>What’s next</h2>
      <p>
        EC/Ed25519 support, batch mode and offline/desktop packaging are on the{' '}
        <a href="/roadmap/">roadmap</a>. The direction is deliberately conservative: every new
        capability ships only when it is proven and documented as honestly as the current ones.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card" data-reveal>
      <h2>About this tool</h2>
      <p>
        CertKit covers the routine certificate jobs that usually end with a file on someone
        else’s server: PFX ↔ PEM conversion, certificate and CSR decoding, key ↔ certificate
        matching, chain ordering and DER/JWK inspection. Every tool is a static page and runs
        on your device.
      </p>

      <h2>Source</h2>
      <p>
        Open source under the MIT license:{' '}
        <a href={REPO_URL} rel="noopener">github.com/pantha704/certkit-mini-saas</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>More tools</h2>
      <p>Other free, browser-first tools from the same workshop:</p>
      <ul class="tool-grid">
        <li>
          <a class="tool-card" href="https://wheelchairrampcalculator.com/" rel="noopener">
            <h3>AccessProof</h3>
            <p>ADA wheelchair ramp calculator and accessibility statement generator.</p>
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="tool-card" href="https://docxcleaner.pages.dev/" rel="noopener">
            <h3>DocxCleaner</h3>
            <p>Strip direct formatting and empty paragraphs from Word manuscripts.</p>
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a class="tool-card" href="https://podlaunch.pages.dev/" rel="noopener">
            <h3>PodLaunch</h3>
            <p>Turn one podcast episode into show notes and a publish checklist.</p>
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="small muted">
        Project location: Kolkata, India. Built and maintained as an independent, privacy-first
        utility.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
